Super user rights are usually restricted to the root account. This means you
will have to log in to a root shell to make any changes to your system. You
could also run commands through su, which does not cache credentials. For this
reason, most systems make use of a helper application that allows regular users
selected by root to run commands with elevated privileges.

BSD's doas has been gaining traction for this purpose on Linux as it is simple
and easy to set up. Its feature set is by design very small and does not offer
much in terms of customization. The more established sudo falls on the other
side of the scale, offering more features than most home users will probably
ever need.

This script uses sudo-rs, a drop-in and memory safe replacement for sudo written
in Rust. sudo-rs is a more modern alternative to sudo that offers greater
customization options than doas. We will use a common usage pattern when setting
up sudo: all users belonging to the wheel group will be able to run any command
with elevated privileges but will be asked to enter their password upon running
sudo.
